{
  "folio": "echo-material",
  "version": 1,
  "phase": "4.1",
  "notation": {
    "what": "The calls for Lesson 4.1 (the echo). Material only: what the folio should PLAY. This file is deliberately schema-neutral — it is not the echo engine's storage format (that lives in briefs/echo.md, written by another hand). Encode from here into whatever the engine wants.",
    "rung": "One drill on the ladder. Ordered. Each carries a seeded key and tempo, a stage, and several candidate calls so a repeat attempt can draw a fresh one.",
    "stage": "contour = answer the shape only (up/down/again). degrees = answer the pitches, all lengths equal. rhythm = one fixed pitch, answer the lengths and the gaps. degrees+rhythm = both, the closing rung. Never all three asked as three separate problems at once.",
    "tonicOctave": "Which octave the tonic sits in for these calls. deg 0 is that tonic. Absolute pitch = tonic pitch class in that octave, then walk the seeded scale. Every call here lands inside MIDI 36-84 (C2-C6).",
    "events": "The call, in order. Each event is either a sounding note {deg,len} or a silence {rest:true,len}. deg is a scale degree relative to the tonic: 0 tonic, 1..6 the scale steps above it, 7 the tonic an octave up, -1 the scale step below the tonic (in a major key that is the leading tone). No chromatics anywhere in 4.1. len is in steps (sixteenths); 1 is the plain step, larger is a written hold.",
    "steps": "Total length of the call in steps: the sum of every event's len. Never more than 16 — the page is sixteen steps.",
    "shape": "The contour of the sounding notes, one character per move, so n notes give n-1 characters: u up, d down, s again (same pitch). Derived, kept here so a contour-stage check needs no arithmetic. null where the call is one pitch throughout.",
    "voice": "Every call is the lead voice alone. The bass is silent in 4.1.",
    "lengths": "Ruling 2026-08-11: the contour and degrees rungs write every note len 1, on the odd grid positions (steps 0,2,4,...) - length is a tool the ear is not being asked about yet, so the calls do not use it. Only one-note-drum and the-sentence carry written lengths, because there length is the question."
  },
